<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page1-what-is-a-game" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>What is an Extensive Form Game?</title>

  <p>
    This is our first example of a <em>game</em> in the mathematical sense: two
    people, each trying to do the best for themselves, where what's "best"
    depends on what the other person does. It looks like a simple story about
    a fender-bender, but the way we turn it into a diagram, a
    <term>game tree</term>, is a structure we will use for the rest of
    the semester. Pay close attention to how the story becomes a picture.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-1-1">
    <title>Section 1.1, Tony's Accident</title>
    <p>
      Read Section 1.1 of <em>Game Theory in Action</em>.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <task xml:id="reflect-1-1" type="reflection">
    <title>Pause and Reflect</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Before you read on in the book: if you were advising Tony after the
        first game tree (Figure 1.1), what would you tell him to do?
      </p>
      <p>
        Now that you've seen how the book resolves it, Tony is out
        $80 no matter what he does, does that match your instinct?
        The authors write, "We did not like this game." What do you think is
        bugging them about it, if anything is bugging you too?
      </p>
      <p>
        Write a few sentences. There's no single right answer here, this is
        about noticing your own intuition before the math takes over.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-1-2">
    <title>Section 1.2, Games in Extensive Form with Complete Information</title>
    <p>
      Read Section 1.2.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    All of this is really just formalizing the type of game we're studying
    right now: an <em>extensive form game with complete information</em>.
    Here's the informal version, which is honestly the important part.
  </p>

  <p>
    "Extensive form" is really just the name for representing a game as a
    <em>tree</em>, the same kind you just saw in Figure 1.2. There's a
    single starting point, the root node, where the first player to move
    makes their choice. Each choice branches out to a new point in the
    tree, sometimes another player's turn, sometimes the end of the game.
    Every branch eventually dead-ends at a terminal node, where the game is
    over and everyone gets their payoff. Because it's a tree, there's never
    any ambiguity about how you got somewhere: if you know where you are,
    you know the exact sequence of choices that got you there.
  </p>

  <p>
    "Complete information" means everyone can see the entire tree, at every
    point, not just their own branch, not just their own payoffs. Nobody is
    missing information about the structure of the game or what's at stake
    for anyone else. (That's different from not knowing what your opponent
    will actually choose, that's the whole point of playing a game, and
    it's still totally allowed under complete information. Complete
    information just means you know the rules and the stakes, not that you
    can read minds.)
  </p>

  <p>
    To make that concrete: imagine Tony has no idea whether the repair is
    actually worth $20 to Vic or $200. Tony can still see the tree's
    <em>shape</em>, the actions, the order of play, but he can't fill in
    Vic's payoffs with any real confidence. That's a game with
    <em>incomplete</em> information. It's still a perfectly good tree, and
    Tony and Vic are still playing a real game, but Tony is missing a piece
    of the picture that complete information would have handed him for
    free. (This is exactly the kind of situation Chapter 4 is built
    around.)
  </p>

  <p>
    That's really the whole idea. Everything below is vocabulary for
    talking about the pieces of a tree precisely, worth having on hand for
    reference, but the big picture is just: draw the tree, make sure
    everyone can see the whole thing.
  </p>

  <p>
    Below is the second game tree from the book (Figure 1.2), where Tony has
    a third option: send $40 up front and the rest once Vic provides a
    receipt. Use the buttons to explore what the different pieces of the
    tree are called.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-tree-toggle-1-1">
    <caption>
      An interactive version of Figure 1.2. Toggle between plain view, root
      and terminal nodes, a sample path through the tree, and the payoffs
      at each terminal node.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-tree-toggle-1-1"
      iframe="ch1-graph/tree-toggle-1-1.html" width="100%" aspect="9:5">
      <static>
        <p>
          A game tree for Tony's Accident with three of Tony's actions at
          the root (send $80, demand receipt, send $40), Vic responding
          with repair/don't repair after "demand receipt" and after "send
          $40," and payoffs (&#8722;80, 80), (&#8722;80, 20), (0, 0),
          (&#8722;80, 20), (&#8722;40, 40) at the five terminal nodes, in
          that order.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    For reference, here's the precise vocabulary from 1.2, tied back to the
    diagram above.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-vocab-accordion-1-2">
    <caption>Key vocabulary from Section 1.2.</caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-vocab-accordion-1-2"
      iframe="ch1-graph/vocab-accordion-1-2.html" width="100%" aspect="3:2">
      <static>
        <p>
          A list of five vocabulary items with definitions: players, nodes,
